course: history
lesson: 38
title: The United States: a short story
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Native nations had lived across North America for thousands of years when, in the 1600s and 1700s, English settlers founded thirteen colonies along the east coast, from Massachusetts to Georgia, on Native land.
explain: The colonists grew angry that Britain taxed them though they had no say in its Parliament, like being charged for a school trip you were never allowed to vote on, and in 1773 protesters tipped British tea into Boston harbour.
explain: On 4 July 1776 they declared independence, saying all men are created equal, though slavery went on, and George Washington led their army to victory and became the first president.
explain: The young country spread west to the Pacific, taking land from Native nations, and welcomed millions of immigrants who sailed past the Statue of Liberty.
explain: After two World Wars the USA became one of the most powerful countries on Earth, and in 2008 Barack Obama was elected its first Black president.
fact: In the 1600s and 1700s English settlers founded thirteen colonies on America's east coast, from Massachusetts to Georgia, on land where Native nations already lived.
fact: The first lasting English colony was Jamestown in 1607, the Pilgrims came on the Mayflower in 1620, and the Spanish town of St. Augustine in Florida is older still, from 1565.
fact: In 1621 the Pilgrims and the Wampanoag people shared a harvest feast; the Wampanoag had helped the settlers survive by teaching them to grow corn.
fact: Americans celebrate Thanksgiving every November, and for many Native people it is also a day of mourning for what followed.
fact: The colonists were angry that Britain taxed them though they had no say in Parliament, and their cry was no taxation without representation; in the Boston Tea Party of 1773 protesters threw tea into the harbour.
fact: On 4 July 1776 the colonies declared independence, saying all men are created equal, with rights to life, liberty and the pursuit of happiness, though slavery continued and women and Native people did not get equal rights.
fact: George Washington led the American army to victory and became the first president in 1789, and he stepped down after two terms, showing that a president should not rule for life like a king, which the founders feared.
fact: The US Constitution of 1787 set up a president, Congress and courts that check each other's power, and it is the oldest written national constitution still in use.
fact: In the 1800s the USA spread west to the Pacific with wagon trains, gold rushes and railways, but this meant taking land from Native nations.
fact: Millions of immigrants came to America from Europe, Asia and Latin America, many passing the Statue of Liberty, a giant statue holding up a torch, a gift from France, and Ellis Island in New York.
fact: After helping to win both World Wars, the USA became one of the most powerful countries in the world, and its films, music and technology spread worldwide.
fact: In 2008 Barack Obama was elected the first Black president of the United States.
exercise: How many colonies joined to form the United States? | Thirteen | Fifty, one for each state | Three big ones | Twelve exactly
exercise: Which was the first lasting English colony? | Jamestown, in 1607 | Boston, in 1492 | New York, in 1066 | Los Angeles, in 1900
exercise: What was the name of the Pilgrims' ship? | The Mayflower | The Santa María | The Victoria | The Endeavour
exercise: Which people helped the Pilgrims survive? | The Wampanoag | The Aztecs of Mexico | The Māori of New Zealand | The Inca of Peru
exercise: What did the Wampanoag teach the settlers? | How to grow corn | How to build castles | How to print books | How to sail to Asia
exercise: When do Americans celebrate Thanksgiving? | In November | In July, with fireworks | In early February | At Easter time
exercise: What were the colonists angry about? | Being taxed with no say in Parliament | Having far too many holidays in the summer | The cold weather in Boston | A football match with Britain
exercise: What happened at the Boston Tea Party? | Protesters threw tea into the harbour | The king held a grand tea party in London | People drank tea with the Wampanoag | Tea was invented
exercise: On which date did the colonies declare independence? | 4 July 1776 | 25 December 1620 | 11 November 1918 | 1 July 1867
exercise: Who was the first president of the United States? | George Washington | Abraham Lincoln | Barack Obama | Benjamin Franklin
exercise: Why did Washington set a good example? | He stepped down after two terms | He made himself king of all thirteen states | He ruled for life | He refused to be president
exercise: What does the US Constitution do? | Sets up a president, Congress and courts that check each other | Makes the president a king who can never be voted out | Abolishes all courts | Joins the USA back to Britain as a colony again
exercise: What was the cost of the USA spreading west? | Native nations lost their land | Nobody lived in the west | It cost nothing at all | Britain lost Scotland
exercise: Who gave the Statue of Liberty to the USA? | France | Great Britain | Spain and Portugal | Mexico, its neighbour
exercise: Who was the first Black president of the USA? | Barack Obama | Martin Luther King Jr. | George Washington | Abraham Lincoln
test: why'd they chuck tea in the sea, bit of a waste innit | To protest against taxes they had no say in | They didn't like the taste of British tea at all | It was too hot to drink | To feed the fish
test: The Declaration said all men are created equal. Why do historians say the words were greater than the reality? | Slavery continued, and women and Native people did not get equal rights | Everyone was equal from 1776, and nothing had to change after that | Nobody ever read the Declaration | It was written in French
test: Why might a Native American family feel differently about Thanksgiving than other families? | For many Native people it is also a day of mourning for what followed | They have never heard of it, because it is only held in the big cities | It is their only holiday | Thanksgiving is a Native war day
test: If a president could stay in power for life, what risk would that bring? | He could become like a king, which the founders feared | Nothing, a president who stays longer always gets wiser and better | Elections would get more exciting | The courts would get stronger
test: Your friend says Jamestown was the first European town in what is now the USA. Is he right? | No, the Spanish town of St. Augustine is older, from 1565 | Yes, nothing in North America was older than Jamestown | No, Boston was founded in 1066 | No, it was New York in 1200
test: A US court tells the president he broke the law. Why is that possible? | The Constitution makes the parts of government check each other | Courts are above everyone and make all laws | The president chose to obey once | It is not possible
test: Your great-great-grandma sailed past a giant statue holding a torch when she reached New York. Where was she probably going? | Ellis Island, to enter the USA | The Moon | Botany Bay | Ellis Island, to leave the USA for ever
test: A cowboy film shows the west as empty land waiting for settlers. What is missing? | The Native nations who lived there | The trains | The horses | Nothing at all
test: Why did the USA become one of the most powerful countries after the World Wars? | It helped win both wars and grew huge in industry and technology | It lost both wars, but Britain and France gave it their colonies | It was the oldest country in the world | It ruled all of Europe
test: ok so obama 2008, why's that such a big deal | He was the first Black president, in a country that once had slavery | He was the first president | He was the youngest person ever | He was from Britain
exam: Identify the grievance summarised by the phrase no taxation without representation. | Taxation imposed by a Parliament in which the colonists had no voice | The representation of the colonists in London by the chiefs of the Wampanoag | Taxation of the colonists by France | The absence of any taxation whatsoever
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the significance of George Washington's departure from office after two terms? | It established a precedent against the indefinite retention of power | It caused the dissolution of the United States into thirteen separate republics | It restored British rule | It made him king
exam: Ascertain the date upon which the Declaration of Independence was adopted. | The fourth of July, seventeen hundred and seventy-six | The fourth of July, eighteen hundred and seventy-six | The first of July, eighteen hundred and sixty-seven | The twenty-fifth of December, sixteen hundred and twenty
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the westward expansion of the United States. | It entailed the taking of lands belonging to Native nations | It occurred upon wholly uninhabited territory awaiting settlement | It was halted at the Mississippi River | It was undertaken by France alone
